# Odd-weight parabolic cohomology bound at levels three and four

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.parabolicH1_finrank_le_levels_three_four_odd`
(`c2c1a34b-7bfe-4fff-8533-9266b78c666a`), by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08.

Proof: submission `11799c4d-6fad-48ec-b735-c3ed4be0ad7f`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-08 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

For $N\in\{3,4\}$ and an odd weight $k\ge3$, the parabolic cohomology of $\Gamma_1(N)$ with
degree-$k-2$ binary-form coefficients has complex dimension at most twice the dimension of
$S_k(\Gamma_1(N))$. Parabolic cocycles restrict to principal cocycles on every rational cusp
stabilizer, and cohomology is taken modulo principal cocycles. This is the odd-weight part of the
remaining small-level dimension input in the MTT mission. Unlike the even-weight case, this cannot
be obtained from the trivial-nebentypus Gamma0 theorem merely by adjoining the central sign; the
elliptic and irregular-cusp contributions must be treated correctly.

## Explanation of the source proof

# Separating the two small odd-weight levels

Let (k\ge3) be odd. For level four, put (n=k-2>0). The two-generator
cohomology estimate gives

$$\dim H^1_{\mathrm{par}}(\Gamma_1(4),\operatorname{Sym}^{k-2}\mathbf C^2)
\le k-3.$$

Thus the analytic lower bound

$$k-3\le 2\dim S_k(\Gamma_1(4))$$

is sufficient for the desired comparison. This lower bound is the
level-four odd-weight specialization of
[Stein, Proposition 6.6](https://wstein.org/books/modform/modform/dimension_formulas.html).
Its irregular-cusp correction gives \(\dim S_k=(k-3)/2\).
At level three the comparison is retained as a separate theorem, where the
order-three elliptic contribution has not been discarded.

This is a formal reduction, not a claim that the two remaining analytic
inputs have been proved. The standalone algebraic level-four estimate is
submitted separately. The proof has no new mathematical assumptions beyond
the three explicit platform inputs, and its final step is natural-number
arithmetic. No raised resource limit or additional axiom is used.

